---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
---
<LegalLayout title="How We Protect Your Anonymity">
  <p>
    Most anonymous review sites protect your identity with a policy. We protect it by not collecting it.
  </p>

  <h2>No Accounts. No Login. No Profile.</h2>
  <p>
    We don't have accounts. There's no login, no profile, no username. Nothing to link your reviews to each other or to you.
  </p>

  <h2>No Work Email Verification</h2>
  <p>
    We never ask for your work email to verify you. Verification through an employer-controlled system is the fastest way to expose a reviewer, and we've chosen not to build it.
  </p>
  <p>
    We don't require an email at all. You can leave one if you want to be able to correct or remove your review later. That's the only thing it's used for. If you don't, you can still share.
  </p>

  <h2>Thresholds, Not Waivers</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing publishes until five people from a company have shared. Below that, no score, no percentage, nothing. Identity-lens results need eight. These thresholds aren't waived — not for a launch, not for a partner, not for anyone.
  </p>

  <h2>We Never Augment Your Data</h2>
  <p>
    We will never add information to your submission from anywhere else. Not from public sources, not from a company we acquire, not from an email you send us. If we ever change what we collect, it applies going forward — never retroactively to what you've already shared.
  </p>

  <h2>Employers Can't Touch Your Review</h2>
  <p>
    Employers cannot edit, remove, or pay to hide your review. They can dispute factual accuracy through our <a href="/dispute" class="text-coral hover:text-coral/80 underline">dispute process</a>. That's the only channel, and it doesn't include deletion.
  </p>

  <h2>What We Collect</h2>
  <p>
    What we do collect: your review text, your ratings, the company, and — only if you choose to add it — an identity lens. Standard server logs, which we retain for 365 days.
  </p>

  <h2>What Could Still Identify You</h2>
  <p>
    What could still identify you: details in your own writing. If you're the only person in your role, describing your role can identify you. We remove names in moderation, but we can't remove context we don't recognize. Write like the person you're worried about will read it.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
